<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 wave 5. Why the last gesture did not do anything, where the person who tried it is
    looking.

    ⚠ **In the panel rather than a log line.** "I drew a lake and nothing appeared" has exactly two
    causes — an unsaved scene and a write that failed — and both are recoverable in one action. A
    message in a log the author is not reading is the same as no message, which is doc 20's first bar.

    ⚠ **An `@if` over the message's length rather than over a nullable, and the reason is the
    surviving-region rule.** An arm's identity is its predicate; "is there a message" is the whole of
    what the arm depends on, and the message itself reaches the screen through the interpolation
    inside it, which re-runs while the arm survives. Written as `@if (Message is { } shown)` the arm
    would be correct for the first refusal and would keep showing it for every later one.
-->
@component WaterNotice
@namespace Vixen.Editor.Water
@inherits Vixen.Ui.UiElement
@tag water-notice
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive

@if (Message.Length > 0) {
    <water-refusal>@Message</water-refusal>
}

@code {
    readonly Signal<string> message = new(string.Empty);

    /// <summary>What it says, or empty for nothing at all.</summary>
    public string Notice {
        get => message.Value;
        set => message.Value = value ?? string.Empty;
    }

    /// <summary>Takes the message away.</summary>
    public void Clear() => message.Value = string.Empty;

    string Message => message.Value;
}
